  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a multi-gradient optimization strategy with adaptive scaling. The key idea is to:\n- Use multiple function evaluations per iteration and compare performance\n- Have each segment contribute to global gradient estimation\n- Dynamically adjust learning rate based on regional topology\n- Maintain asymptotic consistency while avoiding expensive computations\n\nThis approach directly addresses optimization challenges by:\n- Avoiding local minima through broad gradient perspective\n- Adapting scale based on Hessian estimate\n- Better handling of non-separable functions\n- Using Cauchy-distributed perturbations to escape flat valleys\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresent h as a d-dimensional variable vector at resolution n (set n=560). The objective is:\n\n    C = (2/n) * max_{k} \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nSubject to:\n    \u222b h(x) dx = 1\n    0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x \u2208 [0,2]\n\nThis is discretized as:\n    dx = 2/n, h = [h0, h1, ..., h_{n-1}]\n\nThe repair operator R ensures:\n    h'[i] = clamp(0, 1, h[i])\n    adjust h' so that sum(h') = n/2 * dx * (1/ (2/n)) * n/2\n            \nGradient estimation via score function \u2207_h C is not directly available, so we use:\n\n    \u2207_h C \u2248 (2/n) * sum_k [\u03b4(h)/\u03b4(x) * (1-h(x+k)) + ... ]\n\nFor bounds we use logarithmic barriers to maintain 0<h<1.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize using parent if available, else:\n    h0 = np.random.rand(n) * 2 - 1   progress recovery\n    h0 = repair_vector(h0, n)\n    global_best = h0\n    global_score = eval_correlation(h0)\n\nConfigure members:\n    m = min(20, int(np.sqrt(d))//2)  [\"population size\"]\n    \u03a3 = diagonal matrix with entries estimated as n\n    \u03bc = vector with all components 0 for initialization\n    Mode = \"adapting\"\n\ndef run_adaptive_step():\n    global_best_known = global_best.copy()\n    global_score_known = global_score\n    \n    scores = np.ones((m,)) * -np.inf\n    trials_per_member = ceil(0.7 * t_remaining)\n    repair_costs = np.ones((m,)) * REPAIR_COST\n\n    for i in range(m):\n        for t in range(trials_per_member):\n            if time over:\n                break\n            if repair_costs[i] <= 0:\n                h_trial = perturb_i(i, \u03bc[i], \u03a3)\n                scores[i] = eval_correlation(h_trial) - repair_costs[i]\n            else:\n                h_trial = parental_repair(global_best_known, \u03bc[i], \u03a3)\n                scores[i] = eval_correlation(h_trial) - repair_costs[i]\n\n    Sort and compare to global_best.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nVary:\n    scaling: [\"constant\", \"adaptive\", \"gradient-based\"]\n    rounding method: [\"trim\", \"quantile\", \"affine\"]\n    perturbation function: [\"gaussian\", \"laplace\", \"cauchy\"]\n    average function: [\"arithmetic\", \"harmonic\", \"log\"]\n    repair mechanism: [\"linear\", \"nonlinear\"]\n\nUse adaptive hyperparameters for initial distribution based on repair rate.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach iteration costs primarily the correlation evaluation: O(n^2). For n=560, cost beyond 1e6 ops per evaluation.\n\nEstimate time spending 10-15 iterations for safe estimates.\n\nWe ensure safety by:\n    early_solution = check for significant improvement threshold\n    repair_mechanism fallback\n    history monitoring\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFor any h returned, must satisfy:\n    all(0 <= h[i] <= 1)\n    abs(sum(h) * (2/n) - 1) < 1e-5\n\nThen evaluate c = max_correlation(h) * (2/n)\n\nReturn the validated solution.\n\nThe program uses:\n    numpy, scipy, sklearn for gradient estimation (LBFGS)\n    repair_vector function defined to enforce constraints\n    eval_correlation for objective\n    optim_time_remaining and adaptive improvements\n\nFor unknown parent, invokes direct initial search with repair.\n\nAll calls to objective use dx from full computation.\n\nThe validation happens after repair and before return.\n\nComplete implementation handles all details including restarts when stuck.",
